{"blocks":[["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1200","/= - Arithmetic division and assignment (overloadable via DivAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Arithmetic Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1284","% - Arithmetic remainder (overloadable via Rem trait)",["Operator","Operators","Arithmetic Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1347","%= - Arithmetic remainder and assignment (overloadable via RemAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Arithmetic Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1432","Comparison Operators",["Operator","Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1461","== - Equality comparison (overloadable via PartialEq trait)",["Operator","Operators","Comparison Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1530","!= - Nonequality comparison (overloadable via PartialEq trait)",["Operator","Operators","Comparison Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1602","< - Less than comparison (overloadable via PartialOrd trait)",["Operator","Operators","Comparison Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1672","<= - Less than or equal to comparison (overloadable via PartialOrd trait)",["Operator","Operators","Comparison Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1755","> - Greater than comparison (overloadable via PartialOrd trait)",["Operator","Operators","Comparison Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1828",">= - Greater than or equal to comparison (overloadable via PartialOrd trait)",["Operator","Operators","Comparison Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1914","Logical Operators",["Operator","Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-1940","&& - Short-circuiting logical AND (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Logical Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2000","|| - Short-circuiting logical OR (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Logical Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2059","! - Bitwise or logical complement (overloadable via Not trait)",["Operator","Operators","Logical Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2131","Bitwise Operators",["Operator","Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2157","& - Bitwise AND (overloadable via BitAnd trait) or borrow (&expr, &mut expr - not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2266","&= - Bitwise AND and assignment (overloadable via BitAndAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2345","| - Bitwise OR (overloadable via BitOr trait) or pattern alternatives (pat | pat - not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2457","|= - Bitwise OR and assignment (overloadable via BitOrAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2534","^ - Bitwise exclusive OR (overloadable via BitXor trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2600","^= - Bitwise exclusive OR and assignment (overloadable via BitXorAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2688","<< - Left-shift (overloadable via Shl trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2742","<<= - Left-shift and assignment (overloadable via ShlAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2818",">> - Right-shift (overloadable via Shr trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2873",">>= - Right-shift and assignment (overloadable via ShrAssign trait)",["Operator","Operators","Bitwise Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2950","Assignment and Other Operators",["Operator","Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-2989","= - Assignment/equivalence (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3042",". - Field access, method call, or tuple indexing (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3117",".. - Right-exclusive range literal (overloadable via PartialOrd trait) or struct literal update syntax",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3229","..= - Right-inclusive range literal (overloadable via PartialOrd trait)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3310","-> - Function and closure return type (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3374","? - Error propagation (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3422","@ - Pattern binding (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3468",": - Constraints, struct field initializer, or loop label (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3551","; - Statement and item terminator or part of fixed-size array syntax (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3646",", - Argument and element separator (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3707","=> - Part of match arm syntax (not overloadable)",["Operator","Operators","Assignment and Other Operators"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3763","Operator Overloading",["Operator"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3790","Many operators in Rust can be overloaded through traits",["Operator","Operator Overloading"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3850","This allows custom types to use standard operator syntax",["Operator","Operator Overloading"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3911","Common overloadable operator traits include:",["Operator","Operator Overloading"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-3960","Add, Sub, Mul, Div, Rem for arithmetic operations",["Operator","Operator Overloading","Common overloadable operator traits include:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4025","AddAssign, SubAssign, MulAssign, DivAssign, RemAssign for assignment operations",["Operator","Operator Overloading","Common overloadable operator traits include:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4120","PartialEq, PartialOrd for comparisons",["Operator","Operator Overloading","Common overloadable operator traits include:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4167","BitAnd, BitOr, BitXor, Shl, Shr for bitwise operations",["Operator","Operator Overloading","Common overloadable operator traits include:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4237","Not, Neg for unary operations",["Operator","Operator Overloading","Common overloadable operator traits include:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4276","Deref for dereferencing",["Operator","Operator Overloading","Common overloadable operator traits include:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4307","Non-operator Symbols",["Operator"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4334","Rust also includes many symbols that don't function as operators",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4403","These include symbols for:",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4434","Paths through the module hierarchy",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols","These include symbols for:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4474","Generic type parameters",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols","These include symbols for:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4503","Trait bound constraints",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols","These include symbols for:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4532","Macros and attributes",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols","These include symbols for:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4559","Comments",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols","These include symbols for:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4573","Tuples, arrays, and brackets",["Operator","Non-operator Symbols","These include symbols for:"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4607","Related",["Operator"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4621","Programming/Operator - General programming concept of operators",["Operator","Related"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4693","Rust/Trait - Traits used for operator overloading",["Operator","Related"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4751","Rust/Book/TRPL - The Rust Programming Language book",["Operator","Related"]],["p-028d55979bed72c8.html","source-block-4811","- Language features in general",["Operator","Related"]],["p-401a3796e21de07a.html","source-block-28","Rust/Book/TRPL/ch/04/01 What is Ownership",[]],["p-401a3796e21de07a.html","source-block-76","Summary",["Rust/Book/TRPL/ch/04/01 What is Ownership"]],["p-401a3796e21de07a.html","source-block-87","all Programming/Concept/Heap data must be owned by exactly one variable",["Rust/Book/TRPL/ch/04/01 What is Ownership","Summary"]],["p-401a3796e21de07a.html","source-block-167","rust deallocates Programming/Concept/Heap data once its owner goes out of scope",["Rust/Book/TRPL/ch/04/01 What is Ownership","Summary"]],["p-401a3796e21de07a.html","source-block-255","ownership can be transferred with s, which happen in Rust/Variable assignments and calls",["Rust/Book/TRPL/ch/04/01 What is Ownership","Summary"]],["p-401a3796e21de07a.html","source-block-389","Programming/Concept/Heap data can only be accessed through the current owner, not previous owners",["Rust/Book/TRPL/ch/04/01 What is Ownership","Summary"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-60","Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview",[]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-100","Overview",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-115","Let-chains are a Rust feature that allows pattern matching expressions to be chained together with boolean operators (&&, ||) within if and while condition expressions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-295","Stabilized in Rust 1.64, let-chains enable combining pattern matching, variable binding, and boolean logic in a single linear condition",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-435","They transform deeply nested if let statements into readable, linear chains that short-circuit like normal boolean expressions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-568","Let-chains fuse pattern matching, conditional binding, boolean short-circuiting, scope propagation, and expression-oriented programming into one construct",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-727","Context",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-741","Before let-chains, handling multiple Option or Result values required deeply nested if let statements, leading to \"pyramid of doom\" code structures",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-899","Rust's type system requires explicit handling of Option and Result types, making nested pattern matching common",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1019","The feature addresses the need for more readable control flow when dealing with multiple optional values or validation chains",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1149","Let-chains were stabilized in Rust 1.64 after being proposed in RFC 2497",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1226","Key Principles",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1247","Special expression syntax: let PATTERN = EXPR becomes a boolean-producing expression only inside if / while conditions, not a normal statement",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1404","Pattern matching with binding: Each let clause performs pattern matching and introduces bindings that propagate forward through the condition chain and into the body",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1580","Short-circuiting: Like normal boolean expressions, let-chains short-circuit—if any pattern fails to match, evaluation stops and the condition becomes false",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1748","Forward propagation: Bindings from earlier let clauses are available in subsequent clauses and in the body",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1865","Expression-oriented: Let-chains produce boolean values, enabling them to be combined with && and || operators",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-1987","Mechanism",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2003","In the context of if or while conditions, let PATTERN = EXPR evaluates the expression and attempts to match it against the pattern",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2144","If the pattern matches:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2172","Variables are bound to matched components",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","If the pattern matches:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2219","The expression evaluates to true",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","If the pattern matches:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2259","Evaluation continues to the next clause",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","If the pattern matches:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2304","If the pattern doesn't match:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2338","The expression evaluates to false",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","If the pattern doesn't match:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2379","Short-circuiting occurs (for && chains)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","If the pattern doesn't match:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2426","The condition fails and the body is not executed",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","If the pattern doesn't match:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2480","The compiler desugars let-chains into nested match expressions, but the linear syntax is much more readable",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2594","Example desugaring:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-2724","Is conceptually equivalent to:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Example desugaring:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-3045","Examples",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-3060","Basic Let-Chain",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-3481","Without Let-Chains (Nested Approach)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-3971","Chaining Multiple Patterns",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4147","Comparison to Python's Walrus Operator",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4194","Python's walrus operator (:=) provides similar functionality but is binding-only",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples","Comparison to Python's Walrus Operator"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4282","Rust's let-chains provide binding + structural matching + destructuring",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples","Comparison to Python's Walrus Operator"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4359","Python example:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples","Comparison to Python's Walrus Operator"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4506","Rust equivalent:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Examples","Comparison to Python's Walrus Operator"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4694","Where Let-Chains Work",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4722","✅ if let conditions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Where Let-Chains Work"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4750","✅ while let conditions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Where Let-Chains Work"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4781","✅ match guards (pattern guards)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Where Let-Chains Work"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4821","❌ Not allowed in general expression contexts (e.g., let x = let ...)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Where Let-Chains Work"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-4898","❌ Not allowed in arbitrary boolean expressions outside if/while (e.g., foo && let x = bar)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Where Let-Chains Work"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5001","❌ Not allowed with parentheses (must use if let, not if (let x = y))",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Where Let-Chains Work"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5080","Misconceptions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5101","Let-chains are just pattern matching → False. They're pattern-matching expressions embedded in boolean expression chains, combining multiple concepts",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5261","let in conditions is the same as normal let → False. It's special expression syntax that only works in if/while conditions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5402","Let-chains are the same as Python's walrus operator → False. Walrus is binding-only; let-chains provide structural matching and destructuring",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5554","You can use let-chains anywhere → False. They only work in if and while condition expressions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5662","Let-chains always improve readability → False. They can hurt readability when bindings are logically independent or when error context is needed",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5817","When to Use Let-Chains",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5846","Great for:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5865","Nested Option / Result handling",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Great for:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5906","Validation funnels where each step depends on the previous",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Great for:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-5970","Protocol decoding with multiple optional fields",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Great for:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6023","Deeply nested struct access patterns",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Great for:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6065","Early bail conditions with binding",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Great for:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6105","Replacing nested if let statements that form a \"pyramid of doom\"",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Great for:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6177","Avoid when:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6197","Readability suffers from too many chained conditions",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Avoid when:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6255","Bindings are logically independent (consider separate if let statements)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Avoid when:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6335","You need error context (use match or let else instead)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Avoid when:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6399","The chain becomes too long or complex",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","When to Use Let-Chains","Avoid when:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6442","Mental Model",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6461","Think of && in let-chains as: \"Proceed only if this expression is true — and bind variables if applicable\"",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6576","Each let clause:",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6599","Performs pattern matching",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model","Each let clause:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6630","Stops evaluation if it fails (short-circuiting)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model","Each let clause:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6683","Introduces new bindings into the rest of the chain and body",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model","Each let clause:"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6748","The whole condition short-circuits like a normal boolean expression chain",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6826","If walrus is \"Assign while testing\", then let-chains are \"Match while testing\"",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Mental Model"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6913","Formal Terminology",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6938","✅ Let-chains (official name)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-6977","✅ if let chains",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7005","✅ Pattern guards (sometimes used colloquially)",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7062","✅ Conditional pattern bindings",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7103","❌ Not called walrus",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7129","❌ Not assignment-expression",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7163","❌ Not general pattern matching",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Formal Terminology"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7200","Related",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7214","Programming/Concept/Pattern Matching - General pattern matching concept",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7294","Rust/match - Rust's match expression for pattern matching",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7362","- The Option type commonly used with let-chains",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7432","- The Result type commonly used with let-chains",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-f05e8459decd6374.html","source-block-7502","Programming/Destructuring - Extracting values from structures",["Rust Let-Chains Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-789015a012c39cd9.html","source-block-0","a collection of items Rust auto-imports",[]],["p-789015a012c39cd9.html","source-block-46","String and str",["a collection of items Rust auto-imports"]],["p-789015a012c39cd9.html","source-block-68","Vec<T>",["a collection of items Rust auto-imports"]],["p-789015a012c39cd9.html","source-block-80","Option<T> and Result<T, E>",["a collection of items Rust auto-imports"]],["p-789015a012c39cd9.html","source-block-114","Box<T>",["a collection of items Rust auto-imports"]],["p-789015a012c39cd9.html","source-block-126","essentially the same as doing use std::prelude::v1::*;",[]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-117","What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?",[]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-176","Answer",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-189","My Notes",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-206","Game Engines | Are we game yet.rs?",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer","My Notes"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-318","Short answer: The main native Rust options today are Bevy (largest ecosystem), Fyrox (editor + 3D), Macroquad (minimal 2D), and ggez (comfortable 2D). None is a full Unity/Unreal analogue; pick by dimension (2D vs 3D), tooling (code-only vs editor), and tolerance for API churn.",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-614","Bevy — data-driven ECS engine for 2D and 3D; largest community, plugin ecosystem, and learning resources; still pre-1.0 with frequent breaking releases (~every 3 months). bevyengine/bevy on GitHub · bevy.org",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-892","Fyrox (formerly rg3d) — feature-rich 2D/3D engine with a built-in scene editor; closest to a traditional engine-with-tooling stack in pure Rust. FyroxEngine/Fyrox on GitHub",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-1116","Macroquad — small cross-platform 2D-first framework (raylib-inspired); low friction for prototypes and game jams. not-fl3/macroquad on GitHub",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-1309","ggez — mature lightweight 2D framework with comfortable defaults; smaller scope than Bevy, less architectural ceremony than ECS. ggez/ggez on GitHub",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-1504","Also on the radar: Comfy (minimal, macroquad/ggez-like ergonomics), Piston (older modular ecosystem), Amethyst (archived — historical reference only). Hybrid path: run Rust gameplay against Godot via godot-rust/gdext bindings rather than a Rust-native renderer.",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-1816","How to choose (rule of thumb): ambitious multi-system 2D/3D game → Bevy; need a visual editor for 3D → Fyrox; quick 2D prototype → Macroquad; stable simple 2D → ggez; already committed to Godot → Godot + Rust bindings.",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-591373ba673c3734.html","source-block-2054","5 Rust game engines to consider for your next project (LogRocket)",["What are the main Game/Video/Engines for Rust?","Answer"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-0","rust-script",[]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-16","What rust-script Is",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-46","rust-script is a lightweight tool that allows you to run single-file Rust programs as scripts—similar to how you might use Python, Ruby, or Node.js—without manually creating a full Cargo project.",["rust-script","What rust-script Is"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-258","You can write Rust in a single .rs file with special \"script headers,\" and run it directly with:",["rust-script","What rust-script Is"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-410","It handles:",["rust-script","What rust-script Is"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-426","On-demand compilation",["rust-script","What rust-script Is","It handles:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-453","Dependency resolution via Cargo",["rust-script","What rust-script Is","It handles:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-490","Caching for fast re-runs",["rust-script","What rust-script Is","It handles:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-520","Optional shebang execution (#!/usr/bin/env rust-script)",["rust-script","What rust-script Is","It handles:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-583","Why People Use rust-script",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-616","rust-script makes Rust feel like a scripting language while preserving full Rust performance and tooling.",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-730","Use cases include:",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-753","Utility scripts",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script","Use cases include:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-774","Build scripts and automation",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script","Use cases include:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-808","Prototyping Rust code",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script","Use cases include:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-835","Teaching and examples",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script","Use cases include:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-862","Quick CLIs without scaffolding",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script","Use cases include:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-898","Compared with writing a whole Cargo project, it's dramatically quicker for small tasks.",["rust-script","Why People Use rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-990","Key Features",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1009","1. Single-file executables",["rust-script","Key Features"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1048","Write a script like:",["rust-script","Key Features","1. Single-file executables"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1154","Run with:",["rust-script","Key Features","1. Single-file executables"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1217","2. Inline dependencies",["rust-script","Key Features"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1252","Add dependencies right in the file (much like Rust's cargo-script used to allow):",["rust-script","Key Features","2. Inline dependencies"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1624","rust-script reads these headers, generates a temporary Cargo project, compiles it, and caches it.",["rust-script","Key Features","2. Inline dependencies"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1727","3. Shebang support",["rust-script","Key Features"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1758","You can make Rust scripts directly executable on Unix-like systems:",["rust-script","Key Features","3. Shebang support"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-1951","Run directly:",["rust-script","Key Features","3. Shebang support"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2008","4. Cargo-like experience, minimal ceremony",["rust-script","Key Features"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2063","It automatically:",["rust-script","Key Features","4. Cargo-like experience, minimal ceremony"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2086","Creates an implicit Cargo manifest",["Key Features","4. Cargo-like experience, minimal ceremony","It automatically:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2127","Pulls dependencies",["Key Features","4. Cargo-like experience, minimal ceremony","It automatically:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2152","Compiles and caches binaries",["Key Features","4. Cargo-like experience, minimal ceremony","It automatically:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2187","You stay focused on the code.",["rust-script","Key Features","4. Cargo-like experience, minimal ceremony"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2222","5. Fast re-runs via caching",["rust-script","Key Features"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2262","The first run compiles; subsequent runs are nearly instant unless the script changes.",["rust-script","Key Features","5. Fast re-runs via caching"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2353","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2400","rust-script: Easiest for single-file scripts with inline deps; very ergonomic.",["rust-script","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2487","cargo-script: Similar predecessor; rust-script is generally more up-to-date and idiomatic.",["rust-script","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2586","cargo + fn main: Standard approach, but requires project scaffolding.",["rust-script","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2666","EvCxR (Jupyter/REPL): Great for interactive experimentation; slower for scripting.",["rust-script","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2757","shebang with cargo run: Possible but much heavier.",["rust-script","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2818","rust-script strikes the balance between real Rust and script-like workflows.",["rust-script","How rust-script Compares to Alternatives"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2907","Installation",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-2926","is used to install binary crates (executables) globally, not libraries. Libraries are added as dependencies in Cargo.toml.",["rust-script","Installation"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3123","Execution model",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3145","When invoked:",["rust-script","Execution model"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3163","It parses script headers (dependencies, features, edition).",["rust-script","Execution model","When invoked:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3228","Generates a temporary Cargo project under the hood.",["rust-script","Execution model","When invoked:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3285","Compiles and caches the executable.",["rust-script","Execution model","When invoked:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3326","Runs the compiled binary.",["rust-script","Execution model","When invoked:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3357","The cache invalidates when:",["rust-script","Execution model"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3389","The script contents change",["rust-script","Execution model","The cache invalidates when:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3421","The inline dependency list changes",["rust-script","Execution model","The cache invalidates when:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3461","Toolchain changes",["rust-script","Execution model","The cache invalidates when:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3484","Limitations",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3502","Not ideal for multi-file projects",["rust-script","Limitations"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3540","Compilation is still Rust-fast (but not Python-fast)",["rust-script","Limitations"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3597","Inline dependency parsing has some constraints (header comment formats)",["rust-script","Limitations"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3673","Requires Cargo and the Rust toolchain",["rust-script","Limitations"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3715","Quick Example",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-3735","echo.rs:",["rust-script","Quick Example"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-4007","Run:",["rust-script","Quick Example"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-4068","If you'd like, I can also prepare:",["rust-script"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-4106","A cheat sheet",["rust-script","If you'd like, I can also prepare:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-4124","A comparison to Python / Node scripting workflows",["rust-script","If you'd like, I can also prepare:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-4178","A template for a reusable internal tool script",["rust-script","If you'd like, I can also prepare:"]],["p-017c1291fc410d0b.html","source-block-4229","Examples for specific tasks (file I/O, HTTP, concurrency)",["rust-script","If you'd like, I can also prepare:"]],["p-df96539580c201a5.html","source-block-0","main thing is that statements get semicolons.",[]],["p-df96539580c201a5.html","source-block-48","it's possible to have something which doesn't compile because the last line in the function actually has or doesn't have a semicolon.",[]],["p-df96539580c201a5.html","source-block-184","It needs to have a semicolon if the last line is a statement.",["it's possible to have something which doesn't compile because the last line in the function actually has or doesn't have a semicolon."]],["p-df96539580c201a5.html","source-block-249","It needs to NOT have a semicolon if the last line is a Rust/Expression.",["it's possible to have something which doesn't compile because the last line in the function actually has or doesn't have a semicolon."]],["p-df96539580c201a5.html","source-block-530","examples",[]],["p-df96539580c201a5.html","source-block-541","let y = 1;",["examples"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-0","Result<T, E>",[]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-15","A fundamental Programming/Enum in Rust used for error handling, representing either success or failure",["Result<T, E>"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-129","Part of Rust/prelude, so it's automatically imported",["Result<T, E>"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-189","Defined in std::result::Result",["Result<T, E>"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-225","Definition",["Result<T, E>"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-323","T - the type of the value in the Ok variant",["Result<T, E>","Definition"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-375","E - the type of the error in the Err variant",["Result<T, E>","Definition"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-428","Variants",["Result<T, E>"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-443","Ok(T)",["Result<T, E>","Variants"]],["p-2d39b6256fc3780d.html","source-block-457","Represents a successful operation",["Result<T, E>","Variants","Ok(T)"]]],"format_version":2}